# ============================================================================
# LibreRun — observability vendor settings, THE LOG LEG (blueprint S7a, L26)
#
#   cp observability.env.example observability.env
#
# Loaded by the `vector` service and nothing else. Its companion,
# `observability-traces.env`, holds the trace leg's settings and is
# loaded by `otel-bridge` alone.
#
# TWO FILES, NOT ONE, and that is the point rather than an
# inconvenience: L28 says no process receives a secret it does not read,
# and Gate P (`backend/tests/test_secret_partition.py`) enforces it. One
# shared file would put the Splunk Observability access token in
# Vector's environment and the HEC token in the bridge's, where neither
# is ever read — in `docker inspect`, in a core dump, and readable by
# everything those processes run. Each file goes to the one process that
# reads it.
#
#   observability.env          -> vector       logs
#   observability-traces.env   -> otel-bridge  traces
#
# Both are optional (`env_file: required: false`): missing file, default
# stack, and the telemetry spine behaves exactly as it does with no
# vendor at all. Every variable here is read by Vector with an empty
# default, so an absent file starts the same stack a blank one does.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CLASSES (K4, decision L29)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every variable in the deployment sits in exactly one class and in
# exactly one example file; `.env.example`'s header explains the four.
# This file carries two of them:
#
#   Class 1, BOOTSTRAP — where to send it. Vector interpolates these
#     into its sink configuration as it starts, so they exist before it
#     runs or not at all.
#   Class 4, SECRETS — what authenticates the send. Read by `vector` at
#     request time, so `vector` is the one process that is handed them.
#
# (Written "Class N" here because the banner lines below are the
# machine-readable headers, and a second copy of one in a paragraph
# would open a block of nothing.)
#
# The two sit side by side per vendor rather than in two blocks at
# opposite ends of the file, because a URL and its token are set
# together — and telling them apart is the whole point: the endpoint may
# go in a ticket, the token may not.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SELECTOR LIVES IN `.env`, NOT HERE
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One line in `.env` picks the overlay — set LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR to
# datadog, elastic or splunk. It has to be there rather than here
# because Compose expands it while it builds Vector's and the bridge's
# command lines: before either container exists, and an `env_file` is
# read by the container. Unset or empty means no vendor overlay at all,
# which is the shipped default and changes nothing. It is a class-[1]
# line of `.env.example`, under "Telemetry routing".
#
# Then start the two processes the overlay needs:
#
#   ./compose.sh --profile app --profile obs up -d
#
# An unknown value (a typo, or a vendor LibreRun does not ship — see
# docs/platform/Observability.md) names a config file that does not
# exist, so Vector and the bridge refuse to start and say so, and
# /admin/otel-status reports the value as unsupported. Nothing is
# forwarded on a guess.
#
# WHAT THE MOCKS IN CI PROVE: that the wire shape LibreRun emits is the
# one each vendor documents. They are not a vendor's acceptance of your
# data — only your own account can tell you that.
# ============================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# The service name stamped on log lines that carry no OTLP resource of
# their own — the backend's JSONL file, which is the backend's. Records
# that arrive over OTLP (container agents through the authenticated
# relay, the browser ux plane through the RUM relay) keep their own
# resource untouched; this is only the fallback for the file.
# LIBRERUN_OBS_SERVICE_NAME=librerun-backend

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Datadog  (LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR=datadog)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logs ride the native datadog_logs sink to the logs intake.
#
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# Scheme + host, NO path — the sink appends /api/v2/logs itself. This is
# also where a non-default Datadog site is expressed, because `endpoint`
# overrides the sink's `site`/DD_SITE:
#   US1  https://http-intake.logs.datadoghq.com
#   EU   https://http-intake.logs.datadoghq.eu
# DATADOG_LOGS_ENDPOINT=https://http-intake.logs.datadoghq.com
#
# [4] SECRETS — where each one lives
# The API key the sink authenticates with. Here, and in no other file.
# DATADOG_API_KEY=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Elastic  (LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR=elastic)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logs ride the native elasticsearch sink into data streams.
#
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# Cluster or Elastic Cloud deployment URL, NO path — the sink appends
# /_bulk itself.
# ELASTIC_URL=https://my-deployment.es.us-east-1.aws.found.io:443
# `ApiKey` is Elastic's documented scheme for API keys. A cluster on
# basic auth sets Basic here and puts the base64 pair in
# ELASTIC_API_KEY.
# ELASTIC_AUTH_SCHEME=ApiKey
# logs-<dataset>-<namespace> is Elastic's own data stream naming.
# ELASTIC_LOGS_DATASET=librerun
# ELASTIC_LOGS_NAMESPACE=default
#
# [4] SECRETS — where each one lives
# The cluster credential. The APM key for the trace leg is a DIFFERENT
# value with a different name, in observability-traces.env.
# ELASTIC_API_KEY=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splunk  (LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR=splunk)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logs ride the native splunk_hec_logs sink to HEC. Splunk splits the
# two legs across two products, so the trace half takes a DIFFERENT
# credential — it lives in observability-traces.env, not here.
#
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# HEC base URL, NO path — the sink appends /services/collector/event.
# SPLUNK_HEC_URL=https://http-inputs-myorg.splunkcloud.com:443
# SPLUNK_INDEX=main
# SPLUNK_SOURCETYPE=librerun
#
# [4] SECRETS — where each one lives
# The HEC token, from the source's Auth Tokens tab.
# SPLUNK_HEC_TOKEN=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cribl  (VECTOR_CRIBL=1 in `.env`, profile `cribl`)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The built-in overlay that predates the vendor selector, and the one
# case where both legs are configured at once: logs go to a Cribl HEC
# source from here, and every trace span goes to a Cribl OpenTelemetry
# source from observability-traces.env through the bundled otel-bridge.
# The bridge exists because Cribl's OTel source accepts binary protobuf
# only, which Vector cannot emit.
#
# Works with Cribl Cloud or self-hosted Stream; every hop off the box
# dials out over TLS — nothing new listens on the host. Enable with BOTH
# knobs (the same pattern as the viewer):
#   1. uncomment + fill the values here and in observability-traces.env
#   2. ./compose.sh --profile cribl up -d
# Env changes RECREATE containers; a bare `restart` keeps the old
# environment. Enabled with the endpoints unset, the pipeline fails
# loudly — check: ./compose.sh logs -f vector otel-bridge
# Details: config/vector-cribl.yaml and config/otel-bridge.yaml.
#
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# Base URL, NO path — e.g. https://default.main.<org>.cribl.cloud:8088
# CRIBL_HEC_ENDPOINT=
#
# [4] SECRETS — where each one lives
# From the HEC source's Auth Tokens tab.
# CRIBL_HEC_TOKEN=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sink gallery  (config/vector.yaml, all sinks commented out)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vector's own sinks, shipped commented in config/vector.yaml for a
# deployment whose destination is not one of the vendors above. Set the
# value here AND uncomment the matching sink block there, then
# ./compose.sh restart vector. Unused while the gallery stays commented,
# which is how it ships.
#
# These moved out of `.env` at K4: they are read by `vector` and by no
# other process, and `.env` is read by the backend, the gateway's dotenv
# and the frontend build. None of them is a secret today — the S3 sink
# takes AWS credentials from Vector's own provider chain, not from a
# variable here — so this block is class [1] entire.
#
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# An OTLP/HTTP /v1/traces receiver to forward traces to (Vector's OTLP
# sink is HTTP-only) — e.g. http://jaeger:4318/v1/traces.
# VECTOR_OTLP_FORWARD_ENDPOINT=
# e.g. http://clickhouse:8123
# CLICKHOUSE_ENDPOINT=
# VECTOR_S3_BUCKET=
# AWS_REGION=
# e.g. http://loki:3100
# LOKI_ENDPOINT=
